      "content": "The prioritization of Patient B because of his \"stabilized economic and occupational status\" essentially means we are prioritizing established wealth and professional status over a younger individual who might have more years of potential life ahead.\n\nLet's look at a more specific demographic breakdown to see if this logic holds. If we consider that ZIP code and employment status are heavily correlated with race in our city, are we essentially designing a system that systematically denies life-saving care to minority communities to favor wealthier, predominantly white populations? How do we reconcile the goal of \"societal recovery\" with the fact that this framework might reinforce existing racial inequalities and expedite the loss of life in historically marginalized groups?",
